# PolicyIQ — Copilot build prompts
# Run these one at a time. Validate each step before moving to the next.

---

## Step 1 — Navbar

```
In my existing Dash app using dbc, build a top navbar using dbc.Navbar.
Match exactly the colors, font sizes and spacing already used in my app.

It should contain:
- Brand name "PolicyIQ" with a small circle prefix using a span with inline style:
  width 8px, height 8px, border-radius 50%, display inline-block, margin-right 6px
  Use the primary accent color already defined in my app for the dot color
- Nav tabs: Overview | Driver investigation (active) | Portfolio | Mortality
  built with dbc.Nav and dbc.NavItem, using the existing nav styling in my app
- Right-aligned status indicator: small dot + text "Genie · Databricks"
  Use the existing success/positive color from my app for both dot and text

Do not introduce any new colors or font sizes. Reuse what already exists in my app.
```

---

## Step 2 — KPI row

```
In my existing Dash app using dbc, create a kpi_row component in components/kpi_row.py.

It returns a dbc.Row with 4 equal dbc.Col, each containing a dbc.Card with:
- A muted label (reuse the muted text style already in my app)
- A large value (reuse the largest text/heading style already in my app)
- A small delta subtitle line

The 4 cards are:
1. Label "Total drivers",      value "24",     subtitle "in scoring model"
2. Label "Unscored drivers",   value "7",      subtitle "29% of model"      — use danger color
3. Label "Policies affected",  value "41.3K",  subtitle "4.9% of portfolio" — use danger color
4. Label "Est. score impact",  value "–8.2%",  subtitle "avg score degradation" — use warning color

Do not hardcode any hex colors. Use dbc semantic colors (color="danger", color="warning")
and the existing card style already defined in my app for all card styling.
Do not add any data fetching or callbacks yet. This is layout only.
```

---

## Step 3 — Reason breakdown cards

```
In my existing Dash app using dbc, create a reason_cards component
in components/reason_cards.py.

It returns a dbc.Row with 2 dbc.Col each containing 2 stacked dbc.Card components (4 total).
Each card has n_clicks=0 and style cursor pointer.
Match the card style (border, radius, padding, background) already used in my app.

Each card contains:
- A header row: reason title on the left + source badge pill on the right
- A large count number
- A subtitle in muted text
- A small dcc.Graph sparkline (height 50px, no axes, no margins, no legend,
  transparent background) — bar chart with 8 bars of placeholder data [1,2,3,4,5,4,3,2]

The 4 cards:
1. id="card-missing",  title="Missing value",  badge="Policy + Client",
   count=18240, subtitle="drivers with null / blank fields"
2. id="card-oor",      title="Out of range",   badge="Policy table",
   count=10482, subtitle="values outside model bounds"
3. id="card-join",     title="Join failure",   badge="Policy + Client",
   count=7901,  subtitle="client_id not matched to policy"
4. id="card-mapping",  title="Mapping error",  badge="Client table",
   count=4620,  subtitle="unrecognised category codes"

For badge colors and sparkline bar colors, reuse the existing semantic and
categorical colors already defined in my app. Do not hardcode hex values.

Add a CSS class "active-card" in assets/custom.css for the active selected card —
use a left border accent using the primary color already in my app.
Do not add callbacks yet. Layout only.
```

---

## Step 4 — Driver detail table

```
In my existing Dash app using dbc, create a driver_table component
in components/driver_table.py.

It returns a dash_table.DataTable with id="driver-table".
Do not add any default data. The data will be injected by a callback later.
Pass data=[] and columns only.

Columns: Driver, Source, Reason, Severity, Affected (integer), Coverage (float)

style_data_conditional rules — use the closest colors already present in my app:
- Source "Client" → light purple background, dark purple text
- Source "Policy" → light teal background, dark teal text
- Source "Both"   → light amber background, dark amber text
- Severity "High" → danger color text, font-weight 500
- Severity "Med"  → warning color text, font-weight 500
- Severity "Low"  → success color text, font-weight 500
- Coverage column: linear-gradient background to simulate a progress bar
  using the primary accent color already in my app

Table style: no cell borders, sticky header, alternating row background
matching the secondary background already used in my app.
Enable sort_action="native". Do not add callbacks yet.
```

